= Programming language — Technical =

== Language definition ==
A programming language can be characterized by a set of syntactic forms plus semantic rules assigning meaning/behavior to those forms.

A complete practical language definition may also specify:
* lexical grammar;
* contextual/static constraints;
* type rules;
* runtime semantics;
* memory model;
* concurrency behavior;
* foreign-function/interfaces;
* implementation-defined or unspecified behavior.

== Syntax ==
Lexical syntax defines tokens; grammar defines how tokens form larger constructs.

Context-free grammars are widely used for syntactic structure, though real languages may include contextual restrictions or macro systems that exceed a simple grammar description.

== Static and dynamic semantics ==
'''Static semantics''' describes properties checked without ordinary execution, such as name resolution or type constraints.

'''Dynamic semantics''' describes runtime behavior.

The boundary between them depends on language design and implementation.

== Formal semantic styles ==
'''Operational semantics''' defines execution through transition rules.

'''Denotational semantics''' maps constructs to mathematical objects/functions.

'''Axiomatic semantics''' uses logical assertions and proof rules to reason about program properties.

These approaches can coexist and support language implementation, compiler verification, and program proof.

== Type systems ==
Type systems can support abstraction, representation discipline, optimization, and safety properties.

Technical topics include:
* type soundness;
* polymorphism;
* subtyping;
* type inference;
* dependent/refinement types;
* effect systems;
* ownership/lifetime systems.

== Implementation ==
A language may be implemented by ahead-of-time compilation, interpretation, bytecode/VM execution, tracing or method JIT, partial evaluation, or mixed techniques.

Therefore "compiled" and "interpreted" should be treated as implementation characteristics unless a language specification requires a particular strategy.

== Semantics and optimization ==
Compiler optimizations must preserve the relevant language semantics. This becomes subtle when languages expose concurrency, weak memory, floating-point behavior, undefined behavior, reflection, exceptions, or externally observable I/O.

